# `bake` pipeline

When a schema-driven feature changes, trace it through schema loading, validation, template DTOs, proto generation, Go generation, JS generation, and any affected templates.

At the envelope validation stage, `Bake` calls `ResolveProtoData` once and passes that result to proto rendering and integrations. Catalog metadata is already resolved by world construction; file-content validation remains a separate pre-output step. Consumers must not repeat schema resolution or tag allocation. Keep integration output ownership, manager lifecycle, framework adapters and client runtime changes separate from schema/codec refactors.

The integration handoff consists of ordered envelope variants/tags/roles, ordered entity state/delta fields with presence/visibility/dirty-bit metadata, payload fields plus targeting metadata, collection/custom-type dependencies and catalog item/key metadata. These are read-only after resolution. Integration decoration uses per-target entity copies; output selection and defaults live in `codegen/integration_outputs.go`, not schema descriptors. Legacy flat DTOs remain supported by explicit adapters, not alternate emitter policy.

If a new field, target, branch, helper, import, or generated method is added in one target, check the parallel Go, JS, proto, and template paths for the same behavior.

Prefer focused regression tests that inspect generated output for emitted names, imports, helper functions, method shapes, tags, and format strings; do not rely only on helper-level logic tests.

Entity, command, and event root `tag` values pin fields in their protocol oneof envelopes. Untagged declarations keep the legacy sequential assignment, explicit declarations are additive, and `EntityRemoved` stays immediately after the untagged entity pairs. Validate protobuf field legality and all implicit/explicit overlaps before rendering; never repair envelope tags in generated output.

Command and event payload fields may retain legacy alphabetical numbering, or every field in one command/event may use `{ tag: N, type: T }` to pin its direct protobuf field number. Never mix tagged and untagged fields in one payload; entity-targeted commands/events reserve tag 1 for `entity_id`.

Be careful with nested formatting in generators: code that emits `fmt` strings or TS/Go source can need a different level of escaping than normal application code.

After schema or codegen changes that affect bake output, verify `go build ./cmd/golem-bake` and targeted generator tests. For cross-target changes, confirm both Go and JS generated outputs still compile.

For shared codec changes, also compile/run generated C# against the standalone Unity codec sources, exercise generated Go full/public state behavior, and distinguish semantic equivalence, exact bytes and deterministic generated source. Do not impose sorted Go map wire order. Run representative generated consumers in temporary directories; a skipped required consumer check is not acceptance evidence. Preserve exact baseline fingerprints even where their current coverage is incomplete.

Retire a configured shared-only bridge with `golem-bake --remove-integration <target>` before removing its integration stanza. Removal checks generated headers and confines known outputs to the project; it never deletes authored adapters or recursively removes an output directory.

The `threlte` target emits raw `GolemThrelte.ts` compiled by the consumer's Svelte toolchain. It requires `js-client`, an explicit forward-slash module-prefix `protocol_import` (normalize a missing trailing `/`), and an output directory outside `js-client` output (whose finalizer recursively compiles TypeScript). Validate these requirements before writing any bake output. Keep empty entity/world/event sections valid in both generated JS and the Threlte adapter.
